      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which all the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, we start by expressing \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\):\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\]\n\nWe can rewrite these expressions to find a relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nNow, we add and subtract these two equations to eliminate \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\):\n\n\\[ x + y = \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\right) + \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\right) = 2 \\]\n\\[ x - y = \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\right) - \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\right) = \\frac{2}{t} \\]\n\nWe now have two new equations:\n\n\\[ x + y = 2 \\]\n\\[ x - y = \\frac{2}{t} \\]\n\nNext, we solve these equations simultaneously. First, add the two equations:\n\n\\[ (x + y) + (x - y) = 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\]\n\\[ 2x = 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\]\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\) into the first equation \\(x + y = 2\\):\n\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\right) + y = 2 \\]\n\\[ y = 2 - \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\right) \\]\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nNow, we eliminate \\(t\\) to find the relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). From \\(x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\), we get:\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{t} = x - 1 \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{1}{x - 1} \\]\n\nSubstitute \\(t = \\frac{1}{x - 1}\\) into the expression for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} = 1 - (x - 1) = 2 - x \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\n\\[ y = 2 - x \\]\n\nThis is the equation of a line. Therefore, the points \\((x, y)\\) all lie on a line. The correct answer is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\)",
